"93_appvm.mst — AppSpec A5: an app in a VM of its own, talking to the display.

 `docs/appspec.md` §3.2. The author's reason, verbatim: *I do like apps running
 in their own VMs and it scales, at least the logic in the app runs in its own
 thread; we have so many cores on systems now, even the toy Snapdragon has
 eight of them.*

 So an app's MODEL, its render block and its handler live in a worker VM of
 its own, off the main thread, on their own core. The UI worker keeps only
 what AppKit requires it to keep: the window, the controls, and the reconcile
 that touches them. That makes the realizer a small shared DISPLAY SERVER
 rather than the place applications live, and it is why two open apps cannot
 block each other however much arithmetic either one does.

 THREE VMS, AND EACH DOES ONE THING (`docs/worker_peer_links.md`):

     primary     spawns app VMs. That is all it does here — it is NOT in the
                 path of a single frame or event.
     app VM      the model, the render block, the handler. Sends FRAMES.
     UI worker   realizes frames; sends EVENTS back. Talks to Cocoa on behalf
                 of VMs, which is the only thing it is for.

 The two ends talk DIRECTLY, because a spawned worker is introduced to the
 display when it is born and holds its link from then on. Relaying through the
 primary was considered and rejected: it has no interest in this traffic.

 THE SPEC IS THE MESSAGE. `Worker pickle:` carries whole object graphs, so a
 frame is a real `AppNode` on both sides — measured before any of this was
 designed: a normalized spec pickles to 689 bytes, arrives as a real node,
 validates, and diffs against its original to ZERO ops. No wire format, no
 parser, and an app's first frame costs the display nothing to accept. It
 works because blocks cannot pickle and a spec holds event NAMES rather than
 handlers — a rule adopted for the differ that turns out to be what makes the
 tree shippable.

 WHAT RUNS WHERE, precisely: the app VM renders; the UI normalizes, validates,
 diffs and realizes. Diffing on the display side is a deliberate v1 choice —
 the author's priority was that the app's LOGIC runs on its own thread, and it
 does. Moving the diff across is a change to WHAT crosses (ops instead of a
 tree) and nothing else, since both sides already hold the whole model."

"────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 IN THE APP'S OWN VM."
Object subclass: AppVmClient [
    <classVars: Tool AppId>

    "Installed by the init source `AppVmHost` ships. From here on this VM
     answers one question and asks none: what does your window look like now."
    AppVmClient class >> serve: aToolClassName <String> as: anAppId <Symbol> [
        Tool := Worker classNamed: aToolClassName asSymbol.
        AppId := anAppId.
        "PANE TOOLS RUN WHOLE, HERE (docs/process_services.md; the cross-VM
         pane design): if the tool installs an AppToolWindow — under the SAME
         id this app was started as — that window opens LOCALLY on the null
         realizer, whose panes get REAL surfaces. The tool's own paint blocks
         then run in THIS VM against local planes, and publish ships the
         plane bytes with each frame. A tool with no install (a plain
         spec+handler tool) keeps the original spec-only path."
        "THIS VM HAS NO GLASS. A GUI-spawned app boots the full image, so the
         late-bound realizer sweep would FIND CocoaRealize here — and realize
         AppKit from a worker thread. Pin the null realizer first: the local
         window is a model with real headless surfaces; the display owns Cocoa."
        AppToolWindow realizerClass: AppNullRealizer.
        (Tool isNil not and: [ (AppToolWindow named: AppId) isNil ]) ifTrue: [
            [ Tool install ] on: Error do: [ :e | nil ] ].
        (AppToolWindow named: AppId) isNil ifFalse: [
            (AppToolWindow named: AppId) open ].
        "A SPAWNED worker drains through `dispatchPending`, so its inbound
         messages arrive at the `onMessage:` handler — unlike the UI worker,
         which is shape-routed. The two halves of this file use different
         hooks for that reason and it is not an inconsistency."
        Worker onMessage: [ :m | AppVmClient handle: m payload ].
        ^self publish
    ]

    "The local window, when the tool installed one under our id."
    AppVmClient class >> localWindow ^ <Object> [
        AppId isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        ^AppToolWindow named: AppId
    ]

    "Every pane's whole plane, as {paneId. width. height. bytes} — the pixels
     the display cannot compute (they are the tool's) shipped beside the spec
     it can. Whole planes, v1: damage-rect subsetting is a recorded
     optimization, not a correctness need."
    AppVmClient class >> paneBlits ^ <Object> [
        | w spec out |
        w := self localWindow.
        w isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        spec := w lastSpec.
        spec isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        out := OrderedCollection new.
        self collectPanesOf: spec body into: out window: w.
        ^out isEmpty ifTrue: [ nil ] ifFalse: [ out asArray ]
    ]

    AppVmClient class >> collectPanesOf: aNode <Object> into: out <OrderedCollection> window: w <Object> [
        | h plane |
        aNode isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        (AppSpec paneTypes includes: aNode type) ifTrue: [
            h := w paneHandle: aNode idOrEmpty.
            h = 0 ifFalse: [
                plane := AppPixels planeFor: h
                    width: (AppRender widthFor: h) height: (AppRender heightFor: h).
                plane isNil ifFalse: [
                    out add: (Array with: aNode idOrEmpty
                                    with: (AppRender widthFor: h)
                                    with: (AppRender heightFor: h)
                                    with: plane copy) ] ] ].
        aNode children do: [ :c | self collectPanesOf: c into: out window: w ].
        ^self
    ]

    AppVmClient class >> tool ^ <Object> [ ^Tool ]
    AppVmClient class >> appId ^ <Object> [ ^AppId ]

    "A FRAME. Sent straight to the display — the primary is not consulted and
     does not see it."
    AppVmClient class >> publish [
        | spec blits w |
        Worker hasUi ifFalse: [ ^self ].
        spec := self currentSpec.
        spec isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        "THE WINDOW'S GEOMETRY RIDES THE SPEC ROOT: the tool registered its
         window at a size the display cannot otherwise know — the registration
         lives in THIS VM. Without it the display guessed 520x640 and the
         sprite editor's 1020x720 layout crushed panes over controls."
        w := self localWindow.
        w isNil ifFalse: [
            spec at: #width put: w width.
            spec at: #height put: w height ].
        blits := self paneBlits.
        blits isNil
            ifTrue: [ Worker ui send: (Array with: #appFrame with: AppId with: spec) ]
            ifFalse: [ Worker ui send: (Array with: #appFrame with: AppId with: spec with: blits) ].
        ^self
    ]

    AppVmClient class >> handle: payload <Object> [
        ((payload isKindOf: Array) and: [ (payload at: 1) == #appEvent ])
            ifFalse: [ ^self ].
        "THE HANDLER RUNS HERE — in this VM, on this thread. Whatever it costs,
         it costs the app rather than the display. A handler that raises must
         not take its own VM down: the app keeps its last frame and the window
         keeps showing it."
        self localWindow isNil
            ifFalse: [
                "The full local pipeline: handler, rerender, and the tool's
                 own paint blocks against local planes — dispatch does it all."
                [ self localWindow dispatch: (payload at: 2) value: (payload at: 3) ]
                    on: Error do: [ :e | Transcript showCr: 'app handler: ' , e messageText ] ]
            ifTrue: [
                Tool isNil ifFalse: [
                    [ Tool handle: (payload at: 2) value: (payload at: 3) ]
                        on: Error do: [ :e | Transcript showCr: 'app handler: ' , e messageText ] ] ].
        ^self publish
    ]

    "A tool that raises in its render block answers nothing rather than dying
     — but it SAYS SO. Swallowing the error silently produces the worst
     failure this framework has: a window that never appears, with nothing
     anywhere explaining why. The transcript forwards to the display, so the
     reason arrives where somebody is looking."
    AppVmClient class >> currentSpec ^ <Object> [
        self localWindow isNil ifFalse: [
            "The local window's own retained spec, kept current by dispatch."
            self localWindow lastSpec isNil ifFalse: [ ^self localWindow lastSpec ] ].
        Tool isNil ifTrue: [
            Transcript showCr: 'app: no tool class to render'.
            ^nil ].
        ^[ Tool spec ]
            on: Error do: [ :e |
                Transcript showCr: 'app render failed: ' , e messageText.
                nil ]
    ]
]

"────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 ON THE PRIMARY — the only VM that can spawn, and the only thing it does."
Object subclass: AppVmHost [
    <classVars: Apps Watching>

    AppVmHost class >> apps ^ <Dictionary> [
        Apps isNil ifTrue: [ Apps := Dictionary new ].
        ^Apps
    ]

    AppVmHost class >> named: anId <Symbol> ^ <Object> [
        ^self apps at: anId ifAbsent: [ nil ]
    ]

    "Boot an app into a VM of its own. `aToolClassName` names a class that
     answers `spec` and takes `handle:value:` — the SAME two messages a tool
     implements for the in-process path, so a tool does not know or care which
     side of a VM boundary it is on."
    "A DEAD APP IS REPORTED AND RETIRED (docs/process_services.md S2): the
     death notice reaches this primary, the watcher names the app, removes
     its entry (so liveCount is true), and says out loud what the display
     will show — the window keeps its last frame, a known corpse rather than
     a mystery. Installed once, on first start."
    AppVmHost class >> watchDeaths [
        Watching == true ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        Watching := true.
        Worker alsoOnWorkerDied: [ :deadId | AppVmHost noteDeath: deadId ].
        ^self
    ]

    AppVmHost class >> noteDeath: deadId <Integer> [
        | gone |
        gone := nil.
        self apps keysAndValuesDo: [ :k :w |
            w id = deadId ifTrue: [ gone := k ] ].
        gone isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        self apps removeKey: gone ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        Transcript showCr: 'app ' , gone printString , ' died — its window keeps its last frame'.
        ^self
    ]

    AppVmHost class >> start: anId <Symbol> tool: aToolClassName <String> ^ <Object> [
        | w |
        self watchDeaths.
        w := [ Worker spawn: (self initSourceFor: anId tool: aToolClassName) ]
            on: Error do: [ :e |
                Transcript showCr: 'app ' , anId printString , ': ' , e messageText.
                nil ].
        w isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        self apps at: anId put: w.
        ^w
    ]

    "Runs ONCE in the fresh VM, before anything else. It names the tool's
     class and nothing more — the class is already there, because a worker
     boots the same world its parent did."
    AppVmHost class >> initSourceFor: anId <Symbol> tool: aToolClassName <String> ^ <String> [
        ^'AppVmClient serve: ''' , aToolClassName , ''' as: #' , anId asString , '.'
    ]

    AppVmHost class >> stop: anId <Symbol> [
        | w |
        w := self named: anId.
        w isNil ifFalse: [ w terminate ].
        self apps removeKey: anId ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        ^self
    ]

    AppVmHost class >> liveCount ^ <Integer> [
        | n |
        n := 0.
        self apps do: [ :w | w isAlive ifTrue: [ n := n + 1 ] ].
        ^n
    ]
]

"────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 ON THE UI WORKER — the display server."
Object subclass: AppVmDisplay [
    <classVars: Frames Peers Previous Installed>

    "The last frame each app sent, and the link to answer it on."
    AppVmDisplay class >> frames ^ <Dictionary> [
        Frames isNil ifTrue: [ Frames := Dictionary new ].
        ^Frames
    ]

    AppVmDisplay class >> peers ^ <Dictionary> [
        Peers isNil ifTrue: [ Peers := Dictionary new ].
        ^Peers
    ]

    AppVmDisplay class >> frameFor: anId <Symbol> ^ <Object> [
        ^self frames at: anId ifAbsent: [ nil ]
    ]

    "THE UI WORKER IS SHAPE-ROUTED, so an unsolicited message arrives at the
     REPLY handler and never at `onMessage:` — the opposite of the app VM,
     and the single most confusing fact in this seam. Chained rather than
     replaced: anything this does not recognize goes on to whoever was there
     before, so installing a display cannot silently eat somebody else's
     traffic."
    AppVmDisplay class >> install [
        Installed == true ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        Installed := true.
        Worker onReply: [ :m | AppVmDisplay receive: m ].
        ^self
    ]

    AppVmDisplay class >> receive: m <Object> [
        | payload |
        payload := m payload.
        "NESTED, not chained: `and:and:` is not a selector, and writing it cost
         a `doesNotUnderstand` INSIDE the reply handler — every arriving frame
         raised, the drain logged it to stderr, and the display showed
         nothing. The frame path worked the whole time; this guard was eating
         the frames."
        ((payload isKindOf: Array)
            and: [ payload size >= 3 and: [ (payload at: 1) == #appFrame ] ])
                ifFalse: [ ^self passOn: m ].
        ^self frame: (payload at: 3)
              for: (payload at: 2)
              from: m from
              blits: (payload size >= 4 ifTrue: [ payload at: 4 ] ifFalse: [ nil ])
    ]

    AppVmDisplay class >> passOn: m <Object> [
        Previous isNil ifFalse: [ Previous value: m ].
        ^self
    ]

    "A FRAME ARRIVED — a real AppNode, pickled across rather than parsed, so
     everything from here is the ordinary in-process path: the window's render
     block answers this, and the reconcile that follows is the same one an
     in-process tool gets."
    AppVmDisplay class >> frame: aSpec <Object> for: anId <Object> from: aPeer <Integer> [
        ^self frame: aSpec for: anId from: aPeer blits: nil
    ]

    AppVmDisplay class >> frame: aSpec <Object> for: anId <Object> from: aPeer <Integer> blits: someBlits <Object> [
        | key win |
        key := anId asString asSymbol.
        self frames at: key put: aSpec.
        self peers at: key put: aPeer.
        win := AppToolWindow named: key.
        win isNil ifTrue: [
            self registerWindowFor: key spec: aSpec.
            win := AppToolWindow named: key ].
        win isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        win isOpen
            ifTrue: [ win rerender ]
            ifFalse: [ win open ].
        "THE APP'S PIXELS (the cross-VM pane design): the tool painted its
         planes in ITS OWN VM; here they are copied — one bulk replace per
         pane — into the display-side surface and presented. The display
         computes nothing about them, which is the point: pixels are the
         tool's, glass is ours."
        someBlits isNil ifFalse: [
            someBlits do: [ :b | self applyBlit: b to: win ] ].
        ^self
    ]

    AppVmDisplay class >> applyBlit: aBlit <Array> to: win <Object> [
        | h plane bytes |
        h := win paneHandle: (aBlit at: 1).
        h = 0 ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        plane := AppPixels planeFor: h width: (aBlit at: 2) height: (aBlit at: 3).
        plane isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        bytes := aBlit at: 4.
        plane replaceFrom: 1 to: (plane size min: bytes size) with: bytes.
        AppRender present: h.
        ^self
    ]

    "The window is a THIN one: its render block answers the last frame that
     arrived, and its handler ships the event back to the VM that sent it.
     That is the whole of a display server — draw the last frame you were
     given, and never wait for the next."
    AppVmDisplay class >> registerWindowFor: anId <Symbol> spec: aSpec <Object> [
        AppToolWindow
            register: anId
            title: ((aSpec at: #title) ifNil: [ anId asString ]) asString
            width: ((aSpec at: #width) ifNil: [ 520 ]) rounded
            height: ((aSpec at: #height) ifNil: [ 640 ]) rounded
            render: [ AppVmDisplay frameFor: anId ]
            handler: [ :event :value | AppVmDisplay send: event value: value to: anId ].
        ^self
    ]

    "AN EVENT GOES STRAIGHT BACK to the app's VM, on the link its frame
     arrived over. Failure is a state, not an accident: an app VM can die —
     that is the point of giving it one — so a send to a dead peer is reported
     and the window keeps its last frame. The window outlives the VM behind it."
    AppVmDisplay class >> send: event <Symbol> value: aValue <Object> to: anId <Symbol> [
        | peer |
        peer := self peers at: anId ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        peer isNil ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        ^[ (Worker new setId: peer)
                send: (Array with: #appEvent with: event with: aValue).
           true ]
            on: Error do: [ :e |
                AppToolWindow report: 'app ' , anId printString , ': ' , e messageText.
                false ]
    ]

    AppVmDisplay class >> reset [
        Frames := nil. Peers := nil.
        ^self
    ]
]
